# BuildKit looks for <dockerfile>.dockerignore before falling back to a
# .dockerignore at the context root, which lets this file live next to
# the Dockerfile it belongs to instead of at the top of the repository.
#
# It is an allow-list on purpose: a deny-list has to anticipate every
# file that must not be sent, while this way anything new is excluded
# until someone adds it. It keeps the context small; what actually ends
# up in a layer is decided by the COPY lines in the Dockerfile, which
# name their directories rather than copying everything.
*
!cmd/
!internal/
!go.mod
!go.sum
